course: everyday
lesson: 313
title: Kind jokes, teasing, pranks, April Fools, illusions and adverts
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A good joke makes everyone laugh, including the person it is about. Playing along with a little brother's silly knock-knock joke is kind; mocking someone's accent or home language is not.
explain: A prank stops being funny when it damages things, frightens animals, or makes people believe someone is hurt or in danger.
explain: On April 1st, newspapers, TV and companies often publish joke stories. In 1957 the BBC showed a famous report of spaghetti «growing on trees», and many people believed it.
explain: Our eyes can be fooled too. In the Müller-Lyer illusion, two equal lines look different lengths because of the arrow ends; a ruler shows the truth.
explain: Adverts are made to make you want things. They show toys at their very best, use famous people who are paid, hide products inside films, hurry you with countdowns, and use loot boxes that work like gambling.
fact: Playing along with a younger child's jokes builds friendship and helps them learn language.
fact: Speaking more than one language is a skill, and mocking someone's accent is unkind; stand up for them or stay with them.
fact: Never damage property in a prank; someone has to clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime.
fact: Never prank animals; they do not understand jokes and get frightened, stressed or hurt.
fact: Many news websites and companies publish joke stories on April Fools' Day, so check the date and think.
fact: Never pretend to be hurt, missing or in danger as a joke; it causes real fear and can waste emergency services' time.
fact: Our brain can be fooled by what surrounds a shape, so measuring gives the true answer.
fact: Adverts show toys at their best, sometimes with special effects; «pictures are for illustration» means the real thing may be different.
fact: Influencers are often paid to praise products; look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership».
fact: A loot box is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you will get.
fact: «Hurry, only today!» and countdowns rush you so you do not think clearly; real bargains rarely vanish in minutes.
fact: Product placement is when companies pay for their products to appear in films or shows; it is advertising hidden in entertainment.
exercise: Your little sister tells you a very silly knock-knock joke for the fifth time. What is the kind thing to do? | Play along and laugh with her | Tell her the joke is babyish | Walk off without answering | Tell her the ending before she can
exercise: Why is sharing jokes with a younger child good for them? | It builds friendship and helps them learn language | It teaches them early on that jokes are rude and best kept to themselves | It keeps them quiet for a long time | It shows them who is cleverer
exercise: Some boys copy a classmate's accent because his family speaks Polish at home. What should you do? | Stand up for him or stay with him | Join in so they don't pick on you | Tell him to stop speaking Polish | Pretend you did not notice anything
exercise: Why is it wrong to mock someone for the language they speak at home? | Speaking more than one language is a skill, and mocking is unkind | Only English should be spoken outside school, so mocking helps him to fit in faster | Accents are always a joke everyone enjoys | It is only wrong if a teacher hears it
exercise: Older kids plan to throw eggs at a neighbour's house on Halloween. Why is this not a harmless prank? | It damages property, someone must clean or pay, and it can be a crime | Eggs are too expensive to waste | Neighbours always find it funny later, once they have hosed the eggs off the walls | It is only wrong if the house is new
exercise: A boy wants to jump out at his cat to film it getting a fright. What is right? | Don't do it, because animals don't understand jokes and get scared or hurt | Do it once only, because cats forget quickly and won't remember who it was | Do it only if the cat is asleep | Do it if the video will be funny
exercise: On April 1st a news website says the government will make the weekend three days long. What should you do first? | Check the date and think whether it is a joke | Share it with everyone straight away | Believe it straight away, because it is on a proper news website | Write to the government to say thanks
exercise: What was the famous BBC April Fools' report of 1957 about? | Spaghetti growing on trees | Cows that could fly | The Moon made of cheese | Penguins living in London
exercise: A friend plans to «go missing» on April Fools' Day to scare his mum. Why is this a bad joke? | It causes real fear and can waste emergency services' time | Mums never fall for April Fools' jokes, so it will just be a waste of time | It is only a good joke after midday | Hiding is boring for the person hiding
exercise: Two lines with arrow-shaped ends look like different lengths. How can you find the truth? | Measure them with a ruler | Look at them from further away | Ask which one looks longer to friends | Trust the one that seems longer
exercise: An advert shows a toy car racing across the room and jumping ramps by itself. What should you remember? | Adverts show toys at their best, sometimes with effects | Toys always work exactly as shown on TV, or the advert would not be allowed | If it is on TV, the shop must refund it | Only cheap toys are shown in adverts
exercise: A popular YouTuber says she loves a new cereal. What should you look for? | #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership» | The number of likes the video has | How long the video is | Whether she smiles while eating it
exercise: Why is a loot box in a game like gambling? | You pay without knowing what you will get | You always get the prize you want | It is free to open as often as you like, so nothing is lost | It only gives prizes to good players
exercise: A website shows «Hurry, only today!» and a timer ticking down. What is wise? | Take your time, because rushing stops you thinking clearly | Buy quickly before the timer runs out, because the price will go up afterwards | Buy two in case it never comes back | Ask the website to add more time
exercise: In a film, the hero drinks a can of a famous soda with the label clearly showing. What is this? | Product placement: the company paid to show it | Pure luck: the actor was thirsty | A mistake the film makers missed | A secret message for the audience
test: Which joke is fine to share? | One that makes everyone laugh, including the person it is about | One about the funny way a classmate talks, as long as he isn't there | One that tricks a dog into a fright | One where you pretend to be badly hurt
test: A new girl in Sydney speaks English with a Spanish accent and some kids laugh. What is TRUE? | Speaking two languages is a skill to be proud of | She should hide her accent so she fits in and nobody notices her | Laughing at accents is just friendly fun | Accents mean someone is not good at English
test: Which prank would cause real harm? | Stuffing a school toilet so it floods | Putting a googly eye on the milk bottle | Saying «Your shoelace is untied!» on April Fools' Day | Swapping the cereal into a different box
test: A boy in Ireland reads on April 1st that the school year will end in March from now on. What is the most likely truth? | It is probably an April Fools' story | Schools have changed their minds overnight | News on April 1st is always extra true | He should stop doing homework at once
test: Why do two equal lines in the Müller-Lyer illusion look different? | The arrow ends fool the brain about their length | One of the lines really is longer, by a tiny bit | Your eyes need glasses to see them | The paper stretches one line
test: A toy box says «pictures are for illustration». What does that warn you about? | The real toy may look or work differently from the picture | The toy comes with a set of free pictures to colour in | Only the picture is for sale | The toy is bigger than shown
test: Why might a famous gamer praise a headset in every video? | The company may be paying her to praise it | Famous people only ever say what they truly think | Headsets are all the same anyway | Gamers must praise their equipment by law
test: A game lets you buy mystery chests for real money that might hold a rare sword. Which is TRUE? | It works like gambling, and games make it exciting so you spend more | You are sure to get the rare sword if you buy at least three chests in a row | It is free because it is only a game | Mystery chests always give better items than shops
test: In a TV show, every character uses the same brand of phone. What is the most likely reason? | The phone company paid for product placement | The actors all happened to own the same phone already | Only one kind of phone exists | The show was filmed in a phone shop
test: lil sis tells the same knock knock joke 10 times | Play along, it builds friendship and helps her learn | Tell her it's not funny and she's too old for knock knock jokes | Ignore her till she stops | Tell her a better one instead
test: kids in class laugh at the new girl's accent | Don't join in, stand up for her or stay with her | Laugh too so you fit in, it's only a bit of fun | Copy her accent to be funny | Tell her to talk normal
test: ad says "only 10 mins left!!" for the toy | It's a trick to rush you, real deals don't vanish | Buy it now or miss out forever | Ask a friend to buy two | It means the toy is rare
test: wanna write on the bus stop with chalk for a prank | Don't, someone has to clean it and it can be a crime | Fine, chalk always washes off in the first rain anyway | Fine if nobody sees you | Fine if you draw something nice
explain: A boy in England pulled a chair away as a joke when his friend sat down; the friend fell, hit his head on the floor and needed hospital.
explain: A girl in Ohio played a prank on her cat with a loud noise; the cat panicked, ran into the road and was hit by a car.
fact: Pulling chairs away, tripping people and pushing them as a joke often end in head or back injuries.
fact: Animals do not understand jokes, and a frightened animal may run into danger.
fact: Teasing someone about who they like (a «crush», a person you secretly fancy) can embarrass and hurt; if teasing goes on after you clearly ask them to stop, tell a teacher.
test: gonna pull his chair away when he sits lol | Don't, he could fall and hit his head | Do it, it's a classic | Do it on a soft floor | Do it if he's laughing
test: lets scare the dog with a firework for a video | No, animals get terrified and can be hurt | Yes, it's funny | Yes, if it's only a small firework and you're quick | Yes, if you hold the dog
test: everyone's teasing the new girl about her accent | Stop and be kind; mocking hurts | Join in, it's just teasing | Copy her accent too | Ignore her too
test: did u hear some boy pulled a chair away as a joke and the girl "landed on her back and couldn't move for an hour". lesson? | Never pull chairs or trip people as a joke; it causes injuries | Chair pranks are only funny if the person lands softly on a cushion or a mat | Only pull chairs from tall people | It's fine if you say it's a joke
test: my nan says she read on April 1st that "the zoo's penguins learnt to fly". what's likely | It's an April Fool story; check the date and think | It's true, it was in the news, so it must have been checked | Penguins can learn to fly | The news never jokes
test: kids say the girl who cried at the prank "has no sense of humour". pass it on? | No, the joke ended when she was hurt; say sorry instead | Yes, she has none | Yes, and prank her again till she learns to laugh | Yes, and post it in the chat
test: April Fools: "I've told Lily you're in love with her and want to marry her!!" — is it a kind joke? | No; jokes about crushes can embarrass and hurt, so say sorry and fix it | Yes, it's April Fools, so anything goes and nobody's allowed to be upset | Yes, if Lily laughs later | Yes, marriage jokes are always fine
test: your mates keep going "ooh, you and Josh sitting in a tree, K-I-S-S-I-N-G!" and you hate it. what can you do? | Tell them clearly to stop, and tell a teacher if they keep on | Cry and stay home | Hit Josh so they stop | Pretend to hate Josh
test: an advert says "buy this perfume and he'll fall in love with you instantly". true? | No; adverts exaggerate, a smell can't make someone love you | Yes, adverts always have to tell the truth, so it must work | Yes, if you buy two | Yes, but only on Valentine's Day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises product placement? | Payment for products to appear in films or shows, which is hidden advertising | A display of goods upon supermarket shelves, arranged so that they catch the eye | A competition to design a product | The delivery of a product to one's home
exam: Identify the principal reason for which animals should never be pranked. | They do not understand jokes, and a frightened animal may run into danger | Animals find pranks tiresome but harmless, and forget them soon after | Animals retaliate against pranksters | Pranks make animals more intelligent
exam: It is given that a countdown in an advertisement announces «Hurry, only today!». Select the response consistent with the established facts. | It is designed to rush the viewer so that they do not think clearly | It guarantees the lowest price ever offered | It is required by law for genuine sales | It indicates the product is in short supply
exam: State the effect upon a person of mockery of their accent. | It is unkind, since speaking more than one language is a skill | It helps the person improve their speech and sound like everyone else | It has no effect upon the person | It is a form of friendly praise
